WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.053
This is the technical deep dive
for Book 06 of the Federal

2
00:00:04.053 --> 00:00:08.312
Modernization Workbook: Data
Platform, SQL Server Identity.

3
00:00:08.312 --> 00:00:13.493
The book moves the SQL Server estate off
Windows Authentication and onto Microsoft

4
00:00:13.493 --> 00:00:17.638
Entra ID, and this session is for
the people who do that work: database

5
00:00:17.638 --> 00:00:21.265
administrators, the identity
and server teams beside them,

6
00:00:21.265 --> 00:00:23.412
and the security staff who sign off.

7
00:00:23.412 --> 00:00:26.363
It runs about fifty minutes, in two parts.

8
00:00:26.363 --> 00:00:28.902
Part 1 is the strategy, in three modules:

9
00:00:28.902 --> 00:00:33.108
why the inheritance must end and
how the estate is rationalized;

10
00:00:33.108 --> 00:00:38.664
the authentication audit, the three
principal types and the engine-authentication

11
00:00:38.664 --> 00:00:43.425
decision; and NTLM elimination,
applications and the closure condition.

12
00:00:43.425 --> 00:00:47.275
Part 2 walks the ten runbooks
in the order a DBA runs them.

13
00:00:47.275 --> 00:00:52.450
For each one: what it does, the gate it
must pass, and the evidence it leaves.

14
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.063
One convention throughout.

15
00:00:54.063 --> 00:00:57.958
April 1, 2027 is the
program's own NTLM backstop,

16
00:00:57.958 --> 00:01:03.540
not a Microsoft deadline, and I'll
say which dates are external.

17
00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.465
Module 1: the inheritance and the estate.

18
00:01:07.465 --> 00:01:08.640
Three slides.

19
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.177
First, what Windows Authentication
in SQL Server actually is,

20
00:01:13.177 --> 00:01:16.202
precisely enough to see where NTLM hides.

21
00:01:16.202 --> 00:01:21.053
Second, the three drivers that end
it, and the 2027 dates that are real.

22
00:01:21.053 --> 00:01:26.097
Third, the inventory, the rationalization
lenses and the retain, consolidate

23
00:01:26.097 --> 00:01:30.827
or retire gate, which decides which
instances enter the migration at all.

24
00:01:30.827 --> 00:01:35.176
The book's whole order, assess
and consolidate, enable Arc,

25
00:01:35.176 --> 00:01:40.160
migrate logins, then eliminate
NTLM, comes out of this module.

26
00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.135
Start with what the engine actually does.

27
00:01:43.135 --> 00:01:46.722
SQL Server doesn't check the
password for a Windows login.

28
00:01:46.722 --> 00:01:49.234
It trusts the Windows principal token,

29
00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:53.709
and Windows Authentication is always
available; it can't be disabled.

30
00:01:53.709 --> 00:01:57.147
With Integrated Security,
the driver hands off to SSPI,

31
00:01:57.147 --> 00:01:59.820
the Security Support Provider Interface,

32
00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.785
and its Negotiate package picks Kerberos
unless one of the systems can't use it.

33
00:02:04.785 --> 00:02:09.853
The client asks the KDC on a domain
controller for a service ticket to the

34
00:02:09.853 --> 00:02:14.244
instance's SPN, which for TCP
names the SQL Server service class,

35
00:02:14.244 --> 00:02:17.285
the host's fully qualified
name and the port.

36
00:02:17.285 --> 00:02:20.597
The engine validates the ticket
with the service account's key.

37
00:02:20.597 --> 00:02:25.272
No password crosses the network, and
Kerberos authenticates both ends.

38
00:02:25.272 --> 00:02:30.010
The table is Microsoft's authentication
defaults, and it's the one to remember.

39
00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.850
When the SPN maps to the correct account,

40
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:37.497
remote connections use Kerberos,
but local connections use NTLM.

41
00:02:37.497 --> 00:02:42.648
When the SPN is missing or the
lookup fails, both use NTLM.

42
00:02:42.648 --> 00:02:46.210
When it's on the wrong account,
authentication fails outright.

43
00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:50.510
That silent fallback is the most
consequential fault in the inheritance.

44
00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:55.522
The engine logs a failed SPN registration
at startup, but not which protocol each

45
00:02:55.522 --> 00:03:00.385
connection used; only the auth scheme
column of the connections DMV shows that.

46
00:03:00.385 --> 00:03:03.524
And none of these paths
produces an Entra sign-in,

47
00:03:03.524 --> 00:03:06.585
so Conditional Access
has nothing to evaluate.

48
00:03:06.585 --> 00:03:09.675
The identities are anchored
in Active Directory too:

49
00:03:09.675 --> 00:03:12.344
a domain account with a
hand-rotated password,

50
00:03:12.344 --> 00:03:17.260
a group managed service account the domain
controller rotates every thirty days by

51
00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:22.035
default, or the virtual account, which
reaches the network as the computer account.

52
00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:25.676
And the SA login, created
disabled in Windows mode,

53
00:03:25.676 --> 00:03:28.873
is often left enabled
as an emergency way in.

54
00:03:28.873 --> 00:03:30.448
None of this was a mistake.

55
00:03:30.448 --> 00:03:35.140
It was correct engineering for a
domain-joined world that no longer exists.

56
00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:38.198
Three drivers end the inheritance,

57
00:03:38.198 --> 00:03:41.540
and each is as well reasoned
as the original design.

58
00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:43.154
The first is NTLM.

59
00:03:43.154 --> 00:03:46.728
Microsoft deprecated every version,

60
00:03:46.728 --> 00:03:51.340
LAN Manager and NTLM versions
1 and 2, in June 2024.

61
00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:56.137
Version 1 is already removed
from Windows Server 2025

62
00:03:56.137 --> 00:04:01.040
and Windows 11 version 24H2;
version 2 still works there.

63
00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.436
Microsoft's next step is to disable
network NTLM by default in the

64
00:04:05.436 --> 00:04:10.240
next major Windows release, subject
to change and with no calendar date.

65
00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:14.158
So the program set its
own backstop: on April 1,

66
00:04:14.158 --> 00:04:19.240
2027 its enforcement phase
blocks NTLM across the estate.

67
00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:20.928
That isn't the completion date.

68
00:04:20.928 --> 00:04:23.525
It's the date by which consolidation, Arc,

69
00:04:23.525 --> 00:04:27.539
login migration and connection
strings must already be done.

70
00:04:27.539 --> 00:04:29.665
The second is zero trust.

71
00:04:29.665 --> 00:04:33.900
M-22-09 requires
phishing-resistant MFA enforced at

72
00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.115
the application layer, with
a device-level signal.

73
00:04:37.115 --> 00:04:41.790
Conditional Access can act only
on an Entra token request;

74
00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:45.102
a Kerberos or NTLM exchange never asks it.

75
00:04:45.102 --> 00:04:48.178
Risk-based policies need Entra ID P2.

76
00:04:48.178 --> 00:04:50.553
The third is the engine's own identity.

77
00:04:50.553 --> 00:04:53.721
When Active Directory stops
being the identity source,

78
00:04:53.721 --> 00:04:58.196
the engine's service accounts lose their
governance foundation, so the engine

79
00:04:58.196 --> 00:05:02.328
needs an Entra identity first, which
on premises comes through Azure Arc.

80
00:05:02.328 --> 00:05:05.977
What's actually due in
2027 are lifecycle dates.

81
00:05:05.977 --> 00:05:11.153
Windows Server 2016 leaves
extended support on January 12.

82
00:05:11.153 --> 00:05:17.793
SQL Server 2014's final
extended-security-update year ends on July 12,

83
00:05:17.793 --> 00:05:21.289
and SQL Server 2016's first on July 13.

84
00:05:21.289 --> 00:05:25.952
SQL Server 2017 leaves
support on October 12.

85
00:05:25.952 --> 00:05:28.659
None of those versions
can take Entra logins,

86
00:05:28.659 --> 00:05:32.390
so they're consolidation
candidates, not in-place migrations.

87
00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:36.767
The dependencies set the
order: assess and consolidate,

88
00:05:36.767 --> 00:05:40.840
enable Arc, migrate logins,
then eliminate NTLM.

89
00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:45.259
Before any authentication work,
the estate has to be counted,

90
00:05:45.259 --> 00:05:48.415
because a twenty-year estate
doesn't know what it has.

91
00:05:48.415 --> 00:05:53.142
The instances that matter most are the ones
nobody manages, so they're discovered,

92
00:05:53.142 --> 00:05:54.623
not retrieved from a list,

93
00:05:54.623 --> 00:05:58.503
with three instruments that are
each blind to what the others find.

94
00:05:58.503 --> 00:06:04.275
A network and directory sweep listens:
the default instance on TCP 1433,

95
00:06:04.275 --> 00:06:09.852
named instances on dynamic ports,
and the Browser service on UDP 1434.

96
00:06:09.852 --> 00:06:14.420
The community dbatools module's
Find-DbaInstance combines those signals,

97
00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.777
after the program's software approval.

98
00:06:16.777 --> 00:06:22.014
The SPN trail is independent: a forest-wide
SetSPN query lists every instance

99
00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:27.252
ever registered for Kerberos, though
not the ones whose registration failed.

100
00:06:27.252 --> 00:06:33.081
And Microsoft's assessment tooling adds
workload and dependency data: Azure Migrate,

101
00:06:33.081 --> 00:06:37.596
the MAP Toolkit, and the migration
component in Management Studio,

102
00:06:37.596 --> 00:06:41.865
since the Data Migration Assistant
was retired on July 16, 2025.

103
00:06:41.865 --> 00:06:43.403
Then three lenses.

104
00:06:43.403 --> 00:06:47.665
Utilization finds zombies: no
connections in over a year,

105
00:06:47.665 --> 00:06:50.840
no restores, a retired owning application.

106
00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:54.965
Retiring them is the fastest way
to shrink the migration surface.

107
00:06:54.965 --> 00:07:01.969
Version is a primary input, because Entra
logins need SQL Server 2022 or later,

108
00:07:01.969 --> 00:07:05.690
and 2008 and 2008 R2
can't upgrade in place.

109
00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:10.487
Dependencies most often overturn
a naive plan: linked servers,

110
00:07:10.487 --> 00:07:15.653
replication, SSIS packages and the
quarterly cross-database query.

111
00:07:15.653 --> 00:07:18.752
The lenses resolve into
one verdict per instance.

112
00:07:18.752 --> 00:07:20.728
Retain goes straight to the audit.

113
00:07:20.728 --> 00:07:24.278
Consolidate moves the workload
and retires the source.

114
00:07:24.278 --> 00:07:28.102
Retire removes the instance
and every login it carries.

115
00:07:28.102 --> 00:07:30.884
Targets are chosen during the assessment,

116
00:07:30.884 --> 00:07:34.698
standalone or availability-group
SQL Server on Arc hosts,

117
00:07:34.698 --> 00:07:39.069
since failover cluster instances
can't use Entra authentication,

118
00:07:39.069 --> 00:07:43.440
or Azure SQL, so authentication is
migrated once, in its final shape.

119
00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.870
And record the constraints honestly.

120
00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:50.633
Module 2: the audit, the
principals and the decision.

121
00:07:50.633 --> 00:07:51.745
Four slides.

122
00:07:51.745 --> 00:07:55.449
What the authentication audit
reads on every surviving instance,

123
00:07:55.449 --> 00:07:59.220
and why its output is a compliance
record rather than a spreadsheet.

124
00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.652
The three Arc-eligibility
categories, and the distinction

125
00:08:02.652 --> 00:08:06.295
between the engine and the
operating system moving to Entra ID.

126
00:08:06.295 --> 00:08:10.357
The three principal types that
replace Windows and SQL logins.

127
00:08:10.357 --> 00:08:13.330
And the parallel run, with
the five decisions that

128
00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:16.870
turn the migration pattern
into enforceable policy.

129
00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:22.352
The audit runs only on what the gate
kept, retain instances and consolidation

130
00:08:22.352 --> 00:08:26.345
targets, and its deliverable is
a per-instance compliance record.

131
00:08:26.345 --> 00:08:31.046
For each host it records every instance,
with version, edition and build,

132
00:08:31.046 --> 00:08:34.870
and the host operating system,
which sets the Arc envelope.

133
00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:40.658
The Connected Machine agent runs on
Windows Server 2012 through 2025,

134
00:08:40.658 --> 00:08:46.646
SQL Server enabled by Arc needs 2016
or later, and the program sets its own

135
00:08:46.646 --> 00:08:52.733
floor at Windows Server 2019, because
2016 leaves support in January 2027.

136
00:08:52.733 --> 00:08:55.521
It records domain-join
state and whether the

137
00:08:55.521 --> 00:08:58.233
Arc agent and managed
identity are present.

138
00:08:58.233 --> 00:08:59.971
Then the authentication mode.

139
00:08:59.971 --> 00:09:02.617
The registry value LoginMode is one for

140
00:09:02.617 --> 00:09:05.745
Windows Authentication
mode and two for mixed.

141
00:09:05.745 --> 00:09:10.355
The audit cross-checks it against the
running instance with the Is Integrated

142
00:09:10.355 --> 00:09:14.894
Security Only server property, because
a mode change takes effect only after

143
00:09:14.894 --> 00:09:19.220
a restart, and any disagreement is an
anomaly to resolve before migrating.

144
00:09:19.220 --> 00:09:25.025
On SQL Server 2025 enabled by Arc it also
reads Is External Authentication Only,

145
00:09:25.025 --> 00:09:28.895
which shows the Disable
SQL Authentication setting.

146
00:09:28.895 --> 00:09:36.332
Every login is classified by its type in
sys.server_principals: U, a Windows user;

147
00:09:36.332 --> 00:09:39.650
G, a Windows group; S,
a SQL login; C and K,

148
00:09:39.650 --> 00:09:46.058
certificate- and key-mapped; and E
and X, Entra logins already present.

149
00:09:46.058 --> 00:09:49.245
The letters are the view's
values, not mnemonics.

150
00:09:49.245 --> 00:09:52.257
There's no type W, and
a query written against

151
00:09:52.257 --> 00:09:55.770
one returns zero rows and
reports a clean instance.

152
00:09:55.770 --> 00:10:00.017
Active SQL logins are a critical
finding, and an enabled SA with

153
00:10:00.017 --> 00:10:04.182
a non-rotated password is the
highest-priority finding of all.

154
00:10:04.182 --> 00:10:08.120
The audit captures the engine's
service account and checks its SPN.

155
00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:13.187
And each run is compared with the last:
a new SQL login, a revert to mixed mode,

156
00:10:13.187 --> 00:10:19.020
or a new passthrough linked server is
drift, and drift is a compliance violation.

157
00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:24.189
The audit's most consequential output
is a category for every instance,

158
00:10:24.189 --> 00:10:28.907
framed around Arc because Arc is the
program's path to Entra authentication.

159
00:10:28.907 --> 00:10:33.880
Category 1 is Entra-auth-ready: the
Connected Machine agent is running,

160
00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:37.223
the Azure extension for
SQL Server is deployed,

161
00:10:37.223 --> 00:10:42.795
Entra authentication is configured and
the engine is SQL Server 2022 or later.

162
00:10:42.795 --> 00:10:47.733
There the audit's job is a precise login
inventory, and migration starts now.

163
00:10:47.733 --> 00:10:51.032
Category 2 is Arc-eligible
but not enabled.

164
00:10:51.032 --> 00:10:55.679
Prioritize by the audit: instances
with many Windows logins,

165
00:10:55.679 --> 00:10:58.932
active SQL logins or
missing SPNs go first.

166
00:10:58.932 --> 00:11:04.039
The deployment is a server action, not a
SQL Server change; when the agent connects

167
00:11:04.039 --> 00:11:08.994
a server with SQL Server installed, Arc
installs the extension automatically.

168
00:11:08.994 --> 00:11:11.804
Once Entra authentication is configured,

169
00:11:11.804 --> 00:11:14.933
the instance becomes
Category 1 on the next run.

170
00:11:14.933 --> 00:11:17.482
Category 3 isn't yet eligible.

171
00:11:17.482 --> 00:11:22.861
An engine older than 2022 can't accept
Entra logins, with or without Arc, so it

172
00:11:22.861 --> 00:11:25.810
upgrades and follows the Category 2 path,

173
00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.846
or carries an exception
with a sunset date.

174
00:11:28.846 --> 00:11:32.440
Failover cluster instances
belong here on any version,

175
00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:35.957
because Entra authentication
isn't supported on them.

176
00:11:35.957 --> 00:11:40.398
And an exception doesn't save an
instance from the program's NTLM block,

177
00:11:40.398 --> 00:11:45.145
which is applied in the operating system's
security layer, not in the engine.

178
00:11:45.145 --> 00:11:49.020
Then the distinction the book calls
the most common misunderstanding.

179
00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:54.364
Layer 1 is the engine accepting Entra
tokens: SQL Server 2022 or later,

180
00:11:54.364 --> 00:11:58.208
not a cluster instance,
plus Arc and the extension.

181
00:11:58.208 --> 00:12:02.487
It doesn't need the server
unjoined or Windows Server 2025;

182
00:12:02.487 --> 00:12:07.570
a domain-joined Windows Server 2019
host can have Entra logins today.

183
00:12:07.570 --> 00:12:12.557
Layer 2 is administrators signing in
to the server itself with Entra ID.

184
00:12:12.557 --> 00:12:18.265
That needs Windows Server 2025 with Desktop
Experience and Entra-joins the server,

185
00:12:18.265 --> 00:12:21.770
so it's a separate program,
sequenced on its own.

186
00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:27.463
This is where the engine stops
accepting Kerberos tickets and NTLM

187
00:12:27.463 --> 00:12:32.495
exchanges and starts accepting
Entra-issued OAuth 2.0 access tokens.

188
00:12:32.495 --> 00:12:39.170
The mechanism is CREATE LOGIN FROM EXTERNAL
PROVIDER, on SQL Server 2022 and later.

189
00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:42.708
The login is a passwordless
external principal in master.

190
00:12:42.708 --> 00:12:46.681
At connection time the client
presents a token whose audience is

191
00:12:46.681 --> 00:12:51.095
database.windows.net, and the engine
validates it and maps it to the login.

192
00:12:51.095 --> 00:12:56.043
CREATE LOGIN looks the principal up
through Microsoft Graph, so the instance's

193
00:12:56.043 --> 00:13:01.295
identity needs Graph read permissions with
admin consent, or login creation fails.

194
00:13:01.295 --> 00:13:08.295
On SQL Server 2025, WITH OBJECT_ID pins
a login when display names aren't unique.

195
00:13:08.295 --> 00:13:11.016
And Entra connections
are always encrypted,

196
00:13:11.016 --> 00:13:14.032
so clients must trust the
instance's certificate.

197
00:13:14.032 --> 00:13:15.070
Three types.

198
00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:19.133
An Entra user login, type E,
named by the user principal name;

199
00:13:19.133 --> 00:13:24.720
UPNs can differ from SAM account names,
so the audit captures that mapping first.

200
00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:27.370
The engine has no MFA logic.

201
00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:32.956
Conditional Access enforces MFA, device
compliance and sign-in risk before

202
00:13:32.956 --> 00:13:37.833
the token is issued, with no SQL-level
prompt and no SQL-level bypass.

203
00:13:37.833 --> 00:13:41.333
One limit: FIDO2 security
keys aren't yet supported

204
00:13:41.333 --> 00:13:44.357
for Entra authentication
to SQL Server on Arc.

205
00:13:44.357 --> 00:13:49.083
An Entra group login, type X, is
the familiar AD group pattern.

206
00:13:49.083 --> 00:13:53.708
With a dynamic group driven by HR
attributes, people gain and lose

207
00:13:53.708 --> 00:13:58.420
access as their attributes change,
with no CREATE USER or DROP USER.

208
00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:00.793
That needs a P1 license per member,

209
00:14:00.793 --> 00:14:05.707
and access is only as strong as the
write controls on those attributes.

210
00:14:05.707 --> 00:14:11.322
And an application login, also type E, for
a managed identity or service principal,

211
00:14:11.322 --> 00:14:14.507
replacing a SQL login
and its stored password.

212
00:14:14.507 --> 00:14:18.107
Conditional Access doesn't cover
these; service principals are

213
00:14:18.107 --> 00:14:22.319
reachable only through separately
licensed workload-identity policies.

214
00:14:22.319 --> 00:14:26.212
On an Arc server the managed
identity belongs to the server,

215
00:14:26.212 --> 00:14:30.310
so one login per server is the
honest unit of attribution.

216
00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:35.510
No Windows login is dropped until
its Entra replacement is validated.

217
00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:38.178
The engine-authentication decision makes a

218
00:14:38.178 --> 00:14:41.135
three-phase parallel run
required in production.

219
00:14:41.135 --> 00:14:44.279
Create: for every migrating
user or group login,

220
00:14:44.279 --> 00:14:48.310
create the external-provider
login for its Entra principal.

221
00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:53.191
Verify each mapping first; a principal
with no confirmed Entra equivalent is

222
00:14:53.191 --> 00:14:58.148
a blocking finding, and building that
mapping is the most labor-intensive step.

223
00:14:58.148 --> 00:15:01.825
Validate: test each new login
with the principal's real

224
00:15:01.825 --> 00:15:05.422
Entra credential before any
legacy login is touched.

225
00:15:05.422 --> 00:15:09.405
Then disable, don't drop, the
legacy Windows and SQL logins,

226
00:15:09.405 --> 00:15:12.710
observe for thirty days,
and only then drop them.

227
00:15:12.710 --> 00:15:15.623
Observation needs evidence, not silence.

228
00:15:15.623 --> 00:15:20.612
Login metadata says what exists; only
auditing says what's used, and Microsoft's

229
00:15:20.612 --> 00:15:25.372
own sequence uses SQL Server Audit with
the successful-login action group.

230
00:15:25.372 --> 00:15:28.894
Disable-and-observe keeps
every cutover reversible,

231
00:15:28.894 --> 00:15:31.135
so a wrong mapping locks no one out.

232
00:15:31.135 --> 00:15:33.760
Around the pattern sit five decisions.

233
00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:36.298
One, consolidation gates migration.

234
00:15:36.298 --> 00:15:38.534
Two, the parallel run is required;

235
00:15:38.534 --> 00:15:43.672
non-production may shorten the window
only with documented owner sign-off.

236
00:15:43.672 --> 00:15:46.464
Three, SQL Authentication is eliminated:

237
00:15:46.464 --> 00:15:51.637
every active SQL login migrates to a
service principal or managed identity,

238
00:15:51.637 --> 00:15:56.975
or carries an exception with a compensating
control and a sunset date, and SA is

239
00:15:56.975 --> 00:16:02.559
disabled and renamed, with emergency access
as a monitored break-glass procedure.

240
00:16:02.559 --> 00:16:07.592
On SQL Server 2025 with Arc, the
Disable SQL Authentication setting

241
00:16:07.592 --> 00:16:12.085
closes the door for every SQL
login at once, without a restart.

242
00:16:12.085 --> 00:16:16.393
Four, pre-2022 instances and
failover cluster instances upgrade,

243
00:16:16.393 --> 00:16:19.425
re-platform or carry a
sunset-dated exception;

244
00:16:19.425 --> 00:16:23.573
connections that can't reach
Kerberos or Entra by the backstop

245
00:16:23.573 --> 00:16:27.722
use certificate-based authentication
as the interim posture.

246
00:16:27.722 --> 00:16:31.739
Five, the per-instance compliance
record is the closure artifact,

247
00:16:31.739 --> 00:16:35.440
so done becomes a queryable
posture instead of a claim.

248
00:16:36.190 --> 00:16:42.227
Module 3: NTLM, applications and
closure, the last of the strategy.

249
00:16:42.227 --> 00:16:43.341
Four slides.

250
00:16:43.341 --> 00:16:47.856
The four connection patterns that
generate NTLM at the SQL layer,

251
00:16:47.856 --> 00:16:52.790
because NTLM is a protocol, not a
setting, and no single switch removes it.

252
00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:57.640
The three phases that do remove
it: discover, restrict, eliminate.

253
00:16:57.640 --> 00:17:00.499
The four-class pattern
for the applications

254
00:17:00.499 --> 00:17:03.127
upstream of the database,
read on two axes.

255
00:17:03.127 --> 00:17:08.530
And the closure condition: five conditions
that must hold at once, and keep holding.

256
00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:14.153
Microsoft has deprecated every version
of NTLM and removed version 1 from

257
00:17:14.153 --> 00:17:18.868
current Windows, but nothing in its
timeline is a deadline for SQL Server.

258
00:17:18.868 --> 00:17:22.239
Eliminating NTLM means
naming every connection

259
00:17:22.239 --> 00:17:25.529
pattern that produces it,
and the book finds four.

260
00:17:25.529 --> 00:17:30.192
Pattern one, a missing or incorrect
SPN, the most common cause.

261
00:17:30.192 --> 00:17:35.617
If the driver can't get a ticket for the
SPN it builds, it falls back to NTLM.

262
00:17:35.617 --> 00:17:40.742
The fix is SetSPN with the dash S switch,
which checks for duplicates before adding.

263
00:17:40.742 --> 00:17:45.892
Two neighbors behave differently: an SPN
on the wrong account doesn't fall back,

264
00:17:45.892 --> 00:17:49.458
it fails, typically with
"Cannot generate SSPI context",

265
00:17:49.458 --> 00:17:54.054
and a duplicate SPN breaks Kerberos
with a principal-not-unique error.

266
00:17:54.054 --> 00:17:56.493
Pattern two, cross-site connections.

267
00:17:56.493 --> 00:17:58.881
The client needs a
domain controller of the

268
00:17:58.881 --> 00:18:01.468
domain that holds the
server's service account.

269
00:18:01.468 --> 00:18:06.860
If port 88 is blocked or DNS doesn't
resolve it, Negotiate falls back to NTLM

270
00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:12.430
where a trust lets the server validate
the account, and fails where it doesn't.

271
00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:15.605
Confirm with klist before
changing firewall rules.

272
00:18:15.605 --> 00:18:19.394
Cloud Kerberos trust isn't a
fix, because its partial ticket

273
00:18:19.394 --> 00:18:23.105
is still exchanged with an
on-premises domain controller.

274
00:18:23.105 --> 00:18:25.605
Pattern three, linked-server passthrough.

275
00:18:25.605 --> 00:18:30.344
Presenting the caller's identity to
a second instance is a double hop,

276
00:18:30.344 --> 00:18:35.167
and NTLM can't make it; the symptom is
a failed login for anonymous logon.

277
00:18:35.167 --> 00:18:41.101
Move each definition to a mapped login,
an Entra identity on 2022 and later,

278
00:18:41.101 --> 00:18:46.256
a managed identity on 2025, or
Kerberos constrained delegation.

279
00:18:46.256 --> 00:18:49.180
Pattern four, clients
with no domain ticket.

280
00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:54.760
An application on an unjoined host still
using Integrated Security falls back

281
00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:59.918
to NTLM or fails, so its connection
string moves to managed identity first.

282
00:18:59.918 --> 00:19:03.789
Connections on the instance's
own host always use NTLM,

283
00:19:03.789 --> 00:19:07.143
and legacy providers over
named pipes force it.

284
00:19:07.143 --> 00:19:13.250
And NTLM version 1 found anywhere is a
critical finding, not a migration item.

285
00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.850
The program's three phases
apply to SQL Server unchanged.

286
00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:19.668
After the April 2027 block,

287
00:19:19.668 --> 00:19:24.625
any connection that needs NTLM fails
in the Windows security subsystem,

288
00:19:24.625 --> 00:19:30.325
where the Local Security Authority enforces
the policy, so the engine never sees it.

289
00:19:30.325 --> 00:19:34.500
Every pattern has to be resolved
before Phase C, not after.

290
00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:35.875
Phase A discovers.

291
00:19:35.875 --> 00:19:39.719
The authentication audit
is the primary instrument,

292
00:19:39.719 --> 00:19:43.563
and Windows logging records
what actually happened:

293
00:19:43.563 --> 00:19:49.154
events 4624 and 4625 with logon type 3
and the NTLM package on SQL Server hosts;

294
00:19:49.154 --> 00:19:55.095
the auth scheme column for per-connection
proof; the Restrict NTLM audit policies,

295
00:19:55.095 --> 00:19:57.890
which log what a later deny would block,

296
00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:01.996
with event 8004 on domain
controllers naming the server;

297
00:20:01.996 --> 00:20:07.675
and enhanced NTLM logging, which says who
used NTLM and why Kerberos wasn't used.

298
00:20:07.675 --> 00:20:09.237
Phase B restricts.

299
00:20:09.237 --> 00:20:13.088
First, the LAN Manager
authentication level set to five.

300
00:20:13.088 --> 00:20:19.974
Be precise: domain controllers then refuse
LM and version 1 but still accept version

301
00:20:19.974 --> 00:20:22.926
2, so level five is the version-1 block,

302
00:20:22.926 --> 00:20:27.451
targeted for the third quarter
of 2026, not an NTLM block.

303
00:20:27.451 --> 00:20:33.177
Microsoft's October 2026 update is a
different control again; it blocks only NTLM

304
00:20:33.177 --> 00:20:39.075
version 1 derived single sign-on credentials,
on devices without Credential Guard.

305
00:20:39.075 --> 00:20:43.111
Then the pattern work: SPNs,
domain-controller reachability,

306
00:20:43.111 --> 00:20:45.775
linked servers and connection strings.

307
00:20:45.775 --> 00:20:49.908
Version 2 is restricted with
the Restrict NTLM deny settings,

308
00:20:49.908 --> 00:20:55.500
whose exceptions are lists of server names
on clients and domain controllers only.

309
00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:58.012
There's no list for the server role,

310
00:20:58.012 --> 00:21:03.125
so an instance that must keep accepting
NTLM can't deny incoming NTLM.

311
00:21:03.125 --> 00:21:08.687
Phase C eliminates: Restrict
NTLM set to deny on every host.

312
00:21:08.687 --> 00:21:12.145
It's safe only after login
migration is complete,

313
00:21:12.145 --> 00:21:16.987
and an instance on Entra logins is
immune, because it isn't using NTLM.

314
00:21:16.987 --> 00:21:19.499
The book leaves one decision open:

315
00:21:19.499 --> 00:21:23.440
whether the date holds for
the pre-2022 long tail.

316
00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:27.740
The database is only half
of the authentication path.

317
00:21:27.740 --> 00:21:31.597
An application that moved its
user sign-in to Entra ID but

318
00:21:31.597 --> 00:21:35.453
still reaches SQL Server with
a SQL login hasn't finished.

319
00:21:35.453 --> 00:21:38.731
The chain runs from user to
application to database,

320
00:21:38.731 --> 00:21:41.552
and both legs have to
leave Active Directory.

321
00:21:41.552 --> 00:21:46.996
The program's LDAP-dependent-application
plan sorts applications into four classes,

322
00:21:46.996 --> 00:21:51.153
in strict preference order; the
numbers are identifiers, not ranks.

323
00:21:51.153 --> 00:21:56.417
Class 3, OpenID Connect, is the
destination: Entra ID issues the tokens,

324
00:21:56.417 --> 00:21:59.096
there's no LDAP bind and no AD query,

325
00:21:59.096 --> 00:22:04.177
and the application reaches SQL
Server with its managed identity.

326
00:22:04.177 --> 00:22:09.637
Class 2, SAML, removes the LDAP
dependency but ranks below OIDC,

327
00:22:09.637 --> 00:22:14.478
because Microsoft recommends
OIDC for new development.

328
00:22:14.478 --> 00:22:18.913
Class 1, Application Proxy, fronts
an application that can't change;

329
00:22:18.913 --> 00:22:23.108
Entra preauthenticates users,
so Conditional Access applies,

330
00:22:23.108 --> 00:22:27.302
but the application's own AD
dependency stays behind the proxy.

331
00:22:27.302 --> 00:22:31.547
Class 4, Entra Domain
Services, is the last resort:

332
00:22:31.547 --> 00:22:35.578
a managed domain with
LDAP, Kerberos and NTLM.

333
00:22:35.578 --> 00:22:41.138
Its NTLM is still NTLM, and an instance
serving a Class 4 application can't

334
00:22:41.138 --> 00:22:46.791
reach the Entra-exclusive posture, so
it carries a sunset-dated exception.

335
00:22:46.791 --> 00:22:49.728
The class describes only the user axis.

336
00:22:49.728 --> 00:22:53.201
The database connection is a
second axis, set by evidence:

337
00:22:53.201 --> 00:22:56.157
does the deployed driver
support the Entra mode,

338
00:22:56.157 --> 00:23:00.738
can the application use a managed
identity or must its code refresh tokens,

339
00:23:00.738 --> 00:23:04.950
does pooling survive per-identity
tokens, does the vendor support it,

340
00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:08.053
and does the platform have
an Entra-capable driver.

341
00:23:08.053 --> 00:23:13.679
That places each application in
column A, configuration only;

342
00:23:13.679 --> 00:23:18.202
B, code change; C, vendor-dependent;
or D, no Entra path.

343
00:23:18.202 --> 00:23:22.305
OIDC or SAML in column A
moves now; B is scheduled;

344
00:23:22.305 --> 00:23:27.127
C gets a dated exception; D
means exception or retirement.

345
00:23:27.127 --> 00:23:31.135
The application audit supplies the
user axis, and it has to be joined

346
00:23:31.135 --> 00:23:35.580
to the authentication audit before
an instance shows its full chain.

347
00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:41.180
A transformation that reaches its end
state and stops monitoring will regress.

348
00:23:41.180 --> 00:23:46.093
Any administrator with permission can
switch an instance back to mixed mode or

349
00:23:46.093 --> 00:23:50.188
create a new login, so closure is a
posture, kept by continuous audit,

350
00:23:50.188 --> 00:23:54.580
drift detection and governance that
treats regression as a violation.

351
00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:57.615
The transformation is
closed when five conditions

352
00:23:57.615 --> 00:24:00.580
hold at the same time on
every production instance.

353
00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:05.300
One: no usable Windows-backed
principal, meaning zero enabled user or

354
00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:10.268
group logins with CONNECT SQL, apart
from a documented break-glass group.

355
00:24:10.268 --> 00:24:16.732
Two: SQL Authentication eliminated: no
SQL logins outside the exception registry,

356
00:24:16.732 --> 00:24:18.823
SA disabled, LoginMode one,

357
00:24:18.823 --> 00:24:23.767
and on SQL Server 2025 Is External
Authentication Only as well.

358
00:24:23.767 --> 00:24:27.480
Disabling SQL authentication
doesn't remove logins

359
00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:30.855
or end live sessions, so
the census still matters.

360
00:24:30.855 --> 00:24:35.376
Three: Entra authentication
observed working, and nothing else:

361
00:24:35.376 --> 00:24:37.594
every usable login is type E or X,

362
00:24:37.594 --> 00:24:42.287
the sign-in logs and SQL Server
Audit show the expected principals,

363
00:24:42.287 --> 00:24:47.917
and the auth scheme column shows no NTLM
or Kerberos over the validation period.

364
00:24:47.917 --> 00:24:52.050
Four: the engine has a cloud identity,
with the Arc agent connected,

365
00:24:52.050 --> 00:24:56.028
the extension deployed and
Entra authentication configured;

366
00:24:56.028 --> 00:24:59.318
a failover cluster instance
can't meet this as built.

367
00:24:59.318 --> 00:25:04.505
Five: every human authenticates with
MFA through Conditional Access.

368
00:25:04.505 --> 00:25:06.267
No single setting does this.

369
00:25:06.267 --> 00:25:09.557
SQL Server can't turn
Windows Authentication off,

370
00:25:09.557 --> 00:25:13.329
so LoginMode one proves only
that SQL logins are refused.

371
00:25:13.329 --> 00:25:17.855
The book calls the combined state the
Entra-exclusive operational posture.

372
00:25:17.855 --> 00:25:20.643
Two details govern how evidence is read.

373
00:25:20.643 --> 00:25:25.601
Every SQL resource shares one token
audience, so a sign-in log can't say which

374
00:25:25.601 --> 00:25:30.880
instance a principal reached; the per-instance
proof is the instance's own audit.

375
00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:36.855
And local connections always report
NTLM, so separate them from remote ones.

376
00:25:36.855 --> 00:25:41.183
Drift is a violation: LoginMode
moving from one to two,

377
00:25:41.183 --> 00:25:45.905
a new SQL, user or group login,
or the Arc agent going offline.

378
00:25:45.905 --> 00:25:51.650
And the final months before April 2027
are for validation, not migration.

379
00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:55.125
Part 2: the runbooks for DBAs.

380
00:25:55.125 --> 00:26:00.740
Module 4 covers the first five: prerequisites,
discovery and the consolidation gate,

381
00:26:00.740 --> 00:26:04.430
the authentication audit,
Arc and the managed identity,

382
00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:07.238
and login migration with its parallel run.

383
00:26:07.238 --> 00:26:10.516
For each, the steps, the
gate that has to hold before

384
00:26:10.516 --> 00:26:13.875
the next runbook starts,
and the evidence it leaves.

385
00:26:13.875 --> 00:26:18.817
One rule runs through all ten: read-only
and derivation functions run directly,

386
00:26:18.817 --> 00:26:22.542
and anything that changes state
plans by default or writes

387
00:26:22.542 --> 00:26:25.963
a reviewable script, then
goes through change control.

388
00:26:25.963 --> 00:26:30.529
The commands are illustrative and
untested against a production estate,

389
00:26:30.529 --> 00:26:33.350
so each runs first in a test environment.

390
00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:38.688
Runbook R01 provisions nothing
and changes no production state.

391
00:26:38.688 --> 00:26:40.701
It sets up what the other nine assume.

392
00:26:40.701 --> 00:26:42.537
Step one, the toolchain.

393
00:26:42.537 --> 00:26:45.476
PowerShell 7 or later is the runtime.

394
00:26:45.476 --> 00:26:50.369
The one external dependency is a Python
interpreter with only the standard library,

395
00:26:50.369 --> 00:26:54.075
which the modules call to compute
the canonical integrity seal.

396
00:26:54.075 --> 00:26:59.576
The discovery Spec scripts are plain
PowerShell, and three modules are imported from

397
00:26:59.576 --> 00:27:05.000
the repository root: SQL Server migration,
identity assessment and plan generators.

398
00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:09.613
Live Azure and Graph operations
need the Arc agent's command-line

399
00:27:09.613 --> 00:27:14.312
tool and the Azure CLI, and run only
behind an explicit opt-in switch.

400
00:27:14.312 --> 00:27:16.438
Step two, the evidence model.

401
00:27:16.438 --> 00:27:21.598
Every derivation and validation function
emits a provenance-sealed envelope:

402
00:27:21.598 --> 00:27:25.507
the schema and artifact type,
the records and summary counts,

403
00:27:25.507 --> 00:27:30.276
and provenance with the module, version,
a UTC timestamp and a content hash.

404
00:27:30.276 --> 00:27:34.988
The seal leaves out the timestamp, so
identical inputs give an identical hash.

405
00:27:34.988 --> 00:27:38.807
Artifacts are reproducible,
and a broken or unattributed

406
00:27:38.807 --> 00:27:42.388
envelope is a provenance-drift
finding by definition.

407
00:27:42.388 --> 00:27:46.732
Write each step's envelope to a
dated folder under version control;

408
00:27:46.732 --> 00:27:49.813
that's what makes Runbook
R09's runs comparable.

409
00:27:49.813 --> 00:27:51.951
Step three, the safety model.

410
00:27:51.951 --> 00:27:55.262
Read-only and derivation
functions run directly.

411
00:27:55.262 --> 00:28:00.335
State-changing functions either plan by
default and execute only with an execute

412
00:28:00.335 --> 00:28:04.437
switch and confirmation, or generate
a script and execute nothing;

413
00:28:04.437 --> 00:28:08.763
the login and group script generators
have no server parameter at all.

414
00:28:08.763 --> 00:28:10.376
No secrets are embedded.

415
00:28:10.376 --> 00:28:14.806
A GCC-Moderate tenant is an Entra
tenant in the commercial cloud,

416
00:28:14.806 --> 00:28:17.900
so its Arc resources
use the AzureCloud name.

417
00:28:17.900 --> 00:28:22.470
Step four, offline snapshots: every
function accepts captured input,

418
00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:26.238
so the chain can be tested
without touching a live estate.

419
00:28:26.238 --> 00:28:29.424
The run order is on the
right, and nothing touches

420
00:28:29.424 --> 00:28:33.680
production until R02 has defined
the retain and target set.

421
00:28:34.430 --> 00:28:40.455
Runbook R02 is Phase 0: count everything
first, decide what survives second.

422
00:28:40.455 --> 00:28:43.937
Discovery is read-only; the
consolidation it leads to

423
00:28:43.937 --> 00:28:47.418
is review-required and
belongs to the migration plan.

424
00:28:47.418 --> 00:28:50.254
The inventory starts from
the program's scripts.

425
00:28:50.254 --> 00:28:53.704
The D1.8 audit script, run
in discovery-only form,

426
00:28:53.704 --> 00:28:59.629
finds instances from their SQL Server SPNs
and an explicit target list and reads

427
00:28:59.629 --> 00:29:03.344
each host's services over
CIM; without a connection,

428
00:29:03.344 --> 00:29:06.793
version and authentication
mode read Unknown.

429
00:29:06.793 --> 00:29:13.626
The D1.5 SPN inventory gives the domain's
SPN trail, with duplicates and orphans:

430
00:29:13.626 --> 00:29:19.093
SPNs on disabled objects, or on
objects with no logon in 180 days.

431
00:29:19.093 --> 00:29:21.286
Those scripts miss what has no SPN,

432
00:29:21.286 --> 00:29:25.988
like an Express instance an installer
dropped on an application server,

433
00:29:25.988 --> 00:29:30.063
so cast wider with Find-DbaInstance,
a forest-wide SetSPN query,

434
00:29:30.063 --> 00:29:33.354
and Azure Migrate or the
Arc migration assessment.

435
00:29:33.354 --> 00:29:37.205
Then the program's estate-inventory
function fuses the sources.

436
00:29:37.205 --> 00:29:42.667
It keys every instance on host and
instance name, so duplicates collapse,

437
00:29:42.667 --> 00:29:48.767
records which instruments found each one,
and applies a first pass on two signals:

438
00:29:48.767 --> 00:29:52.136
last access at or above
400 days means retire,

439
00:29:52.136 --> 00:29:57.143
a pre-2022 engine means consolidate,
and 2022 or later means retain.

440
00:29:57.143 --> 00:30:02.168
Anything without enough signal lands
in review, never silently in retain.

441
00:30:02.168 --> 00:30:04.729
The lenses come next, with one caution.

442
00:30:04.729 --> 00:30:08.397
The index-usage DMV resets
whenever the engine starts,

443
00:30:08.397 --> 00:30:13.205
so it can't prove 400 days of disuse
unless the instance has run that long.

444
00:30:13.205 --> 00:30:18.151
Read the start time beside it, and take
long-horizon evidence from login auditing,

445
00:30:18.151 --> 00:30:21.030
backup history and the
application portfolio.

446
00:30:21.030 --> 00:30:25.327
For dependencies, read
sys.servers and the linked logins,

447
00:30:25.327 --> 00:30:28.892
plus the Agent, replication
and SSIS footprint.

448
00:30:28.892 --> 00:30:34.368
Finally the architects confirm or override
each verdict, with the reason recorded.

449
00:30:34.368 --> 00:30:36.671
The evidence is the sealed inventory,

450
00:30:36.671 --> 00:30:39.942
with its classification
counts and its denominator.

451
00:30:39.942 --> 00:30:43.035
And schedule network sweeps
with security operations,

452
00:30:43.035 --> 00:30:45.820
because intrusion detection can flag them.

453
00:30:46.570 --> 00:30:49.858
Runbook R03 is the audit script itself.

454
00:30:49.858 --> 00:30:53.482
It changes nothing, so run
it first and run it often.

455
00:30:53.482 --> 00:30:58.357
The prerequisites are the ActiveDirectory
module for the live SPN query and the

456
00:30:58.357 --> 00:31:02.722
orphaned-login check, remote CIM to
each host, and SQL Server permissions

457
00:31:02.722 --> 00:31:07.596
only for the deep audit, which connects
with Windows integrated authentication.

458
00:31:07.596 --> 00:31:08.783
Watch one trap.

459
00:31:08.783 --> 00:31:12.145
Catalog views show only what
the caller has permission on,

460
00:31:12.145 --> 00:31:16.769
so an under-privileged audit account
returns a partial login list with no error.

461
00:31:16.769 --> 00:31:19.726
Grant the narrowest read
that sees every login,

462
00:31:19.726 --> 00:31:22.682
and confirm the count
against a known instance.

463
00:31:22.682 --> 00:31:27.953
There are three passes, in order: discovery
only, which opens no SQL connection;

464
00:31:27.953 --> 00:31:31.494
then adding target servers
for hosts without an SPN;

465
00:31:31.494 --> 00:31:35.858
then the deep audit with query
instances and test connectivity.

466
00:31:35.858 --> 00:31:40.581
The output is a JSON file per run,
an instances CSV and a logins CSV,

467
00:31:40.581 --> 00:31:44.057
and the script computes
the readiness verdict.

468
00:31:44.057 --> 00:31:49.496
Major version sixteen and later gets
"enable Entra ID auth via Azure Arc".

469
00:31:49.496 --> 00:31:55.907
2019 and earlier gets "upgrade to
2022 plus Arc", which is Category 3.

470
00:31:55.907 --> 00:31:58.564
The blockers it flags are mixed mode,

471
00:31:58.564 --> 00:32:02.195
an enabled SA, orphaned
logins and linked servers.

472
00:32:02.195 --> 00:32:05.085
It doesn't detect failover
cluster instances,

473
00:32:05.085 --> 00:32:09.420
so flag those from the cluster
inventory and treat them as Category 3.

474
00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:11.732
Two input defects to work around.

475
00:32:11.732 --> 00:32:15.965
The SPN-inventory input file
doesn't match what D1.5 writes,

476
00:32:15.965 --> 00:32:21.696
so passing it yields no instances and
suppresses the live query; run without it.

477
00:32:21.696 --> 00:32:25.807
And the service-account input expects
a script that isn't published yet.

478
00:32:25.807 --> 00:32:34.107
The run order for SQL Server is D1.5 and
D1.7, then D1.8, then the owner matrix.

479
00:32:34.107 --> 00:32:36.893
The script degrades rather than crashing:

480
00:32:36.893 --> 00:32:41.508
a failed connection is recorded as
Query Failed, a slow port as Timeout.

481
00:32:41.508 --> 00:32:44.172
Keep every run under version control,

482
00:32:44.172 --> 00:32:50.200
because the audit JSON is the raw input
to continuous monitoring in Runbook R09.

483
00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:54.400
Runbook R04 gives the
host its cloud identity.

484
00:32:54.400 --> 00:32:58.864
Connecting a server to Arc creates
a system-assigned managed identity

485
00:32:58.864 --> 00:33:03.025
with no secret anyone manages, and
without it there's no Entra login.

486
00:33:03.025 --> 00:33:09.113
SQL Server 2025 uses that identity
directly as its primary managed identity.

487
00:33:09.113 --> 00:33:13.882
SQL Server 2022 authenticates with an
app registration and a certificate

488
00:33:13.882 --> 00:33:18.499
in Key Vault, and the machine identity
is what downloads the certificate.

489
00:33:18.499 --> 00:33:20.712
The prerequisites carry the traps.

490
00:33:20.712 --> 00:33:24.387
A GCC-Moderate tenant's Entra
ID is in the commercial cloud,

491
00:33:24.387 --> 00:33:29.670
so the Arc resources live in a commercial
subscription in the same tenant, and Entra

492
00:33:29.670 --> 00:33:34.724
authentication isn't available for SQL
Server on Arc in US Government Virginia.

493
00:33:34.724 --> 00:33:37.881
The instance can't be a
failover cluster instance,

494
00:33:37.881 --> 00:33:42.729
NT AUTHORITY SYSTEM must be able to
connect or the extension can't provision,

495
00:33:42.729 --> 00:33:45.500
and clients must trust
the TLS certificate.

496
00:33:45.500 --> 00:33:48.837
The agent and extension need outbound 443,

497
00:33:48.837 --> 00:33:54.175
and the engine contacts Entra ID
directly, without the agent's proxy.

498
00:33:54.175 --> 00:33:58.750
That's the one to miss: the agent shows
Connected while Entra logins fail.

499
00:33:58.750 --> 00:33:59.975
Then four steps.

500
00:33:59.975 --> 00:34:03.853
Enroll with a certificate-based
onboarding service principal;

501
00:34:03.853 --> 00:34:07.950
the SQL extension installs
automatically unless a tag disables it.

502
00:34:07.950 --> 00:34:12.916
Validate the token from the local
identity endpoint, a two-call challenge:

503
00:34:12.916 --> 00:34:17.394
the audience must be database.windows.net,
the expiry in the future,

504
00:34:17.394 --> 00:34:20.325
and the object ID the
machine's principal ID.

505
00:34:20.325 --> 00:34:21.700
Never log the token.

506
00:34:21.700 --> 00:34:24.075
Confirm the extension shows Succeeded.

507
00:34:24.075 --> 00:34:27.092
Then turn on Entra
authentication, on a pilot first;

508
00:34:27.092 --> 00:34:30.899
a Privileged Role Administrator
grants the Graph permissions,

509
00:34:30.899 --> 00:34:34.563
and setting the Entra admin
creates the first sysadmin login.

510
00:34:34.563 --> 00:34:38.686
Two program functions need correcting
before you execute with them:

511
00:34:38.686 --> 00:34:43.461
the onboarding wrapper adds a proxy flag
the agent doesn't have and falls back to

512
00:34:43.461 --> 00:34:46.210
interactive sign-in, and
the token validator's

513
00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:48.525
live mode queries the Azure VM address.

514
00:34:48.525 --> 00:34:53.500
Rollback runs in order: remove
extensions, disconnect, uninstall.

515
00:34:53.500 --> 00:34:57.725
Reconnecting creates a new identity,
so permissions are granted again.

516
00:34:57.725 --> 00:35:03.100
On SQL Server 2025, treat Entra
authentication as one-way.

517
00:35:03.850 --> 00:35:07.707
Runbook R05 adds the Entra
login beside the Windows one,

518
00:35:07.707 --> 00:35:11.900
watches both for thirty days,
and disables rather than drops.

519
00:35:11.900 --> 00:35:16.212
Every step that runs T-SQL against
an instance is review-required.

520
00:35:16.212 --> 00:35:20.369
Don't start until the instance
is on SQL Server 2022 or later,

521
00:35:20.369 --> 00:35:24.526
Arc-connected with a validated
token and an Entra admin set;

522
00:35:24.526 --> 00:35:29.277
until the estate is consolidated;
until you have the R03 logins file;

523
00:35:29.277 --> 00:35:32.162
and until clients trust the certificate.

524
00:35:32.162 --> 00:35:35.951
Step one generates the
statements, dry run first.

525
00:35:35.951 --> 00:35:38.901
The mapping from each
Windows login to an Entra

526
00:35:38.901 --> 00:35:41.625
principal is the one
piece of real judgment.

527
00:35:41.625 --> 00:35:46.409
The program's mapping function builds
it from the identity reconciliation,

528
00:35:46.409 --> 00:35:50.131
not a spreadsheet: it maps only
cleanly matched principals,

529
00:35:50.131 --> 00:35:53.700
prefers an Entra group, and
flags every unresolved login.

530
00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:57.887
The script generator then writes
an idempotent CREATE LOGIN file,

531
00:35:57.887 --> 00:36:03.063
with unresolved entries as commented-out
to-do lines, and never opens a connection.

532
00:36:03.063 --> 00:36:07.688
Step two creates an Entra database
user beside each Windows user,

533
00:36:07.688 --> 00:36:13.513
with CREATE USER FROM LOGIN and the same
roles, permissions, schemas and ownership.

534
00:36:13.513 --> 00:36:17.964
Don't convert with ALTER USER WITH
LOGIN; on SQL Server it can't change a

535
00:36:17.964 --> 00:36:22.650
user's type, and the exception applies
only to Azure SQL Managed Instance.

536
00:36:22.650 --> 00:36:25.625
Step three is the thirty-day parallel run.

537
00:36:25.625 --> 00:36:29.850
Capture successful logins for the
whole window with SQL Server Audit,

538
00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:33.200
because a point-in-time query
misses short connections.

539
00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:38.851
The exit criteria: every application
and job also appears on its Entra login,

540
00:36:38.851 --> 00:36:43.875
no remote NTLM remains, and nothing
arrives only on the legacy login.

541
00:36:43.875 --> 00:36:47.074
Two cautions: local
connections always report NTLM,

542
00:36:47.074 --> 00:36:52.207
and Microsoft doesn't document what an
Entra connection reports in the auth scheme

543
00:36:52.207 --> 00:36:54.216
column, so confirm it on the pilot,

544
00:36:54.216 --> 00:36:57.787
and pre-filter local sessions
before the readiness check.

545
00:36:57.787 --> 00:37:01.698
Step four cuts over by disabling,
including any group logins

546
00:37:01.698 --> 00:37:05.687
a principal also arrives through,
and rollback is re-enabling.

547
00:37:05.687 --> 00:37:11.880
And close an enabled SA here: confirm another
sysadmin can sign in, then disable it.

548
00:37:12.630 --> 00:37:17.875
Module 5 covers the last five runbooks:
NTLM detection and remediation; groups,

549
00:37:17.875 --> 00:37:21.990
administrative units and the
Conditional Access perimeter;

550
00:37:21.990 --> 00:37:25.137
the application chain
and connection strings;

551
00:37:25.137 --> 00:37:30.705
continuous monitoring and the closure
record; and certificate discovery for AD CS.

552
00:37:30.705 --> 00:37:32.005
The pattern holds.

553
00:37:32.005 --> 00:37:37.209
Detection and inventory are read-only
and script-backed, and every remediation,

554
00:37:37.209 --> 00:37:41.073
provisioning or configuration
change is review-required,

555
00:37:41.073 --> 00:37:43.517
dry run first, through change control.

556
00:37:43.517 --> 00:37:47.126
By the end of this module the
posture isn't just reached;

557
00:37:47.126 --> 00:37:49.890
it's provable on every scheduled run.

558
00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:54.957
Runbook R06 is detect, then
remediate, and most of what you find

559
00:37:54.957 --> 00:37:59.190
is a missing or misplaced name
rather than a protocol choice.

560
00:37:59.190 --> 00:38:01.777
Detection is three read-only audits.

561
00:38:01.777 --> 00:38:06.853
The D1.5 SPN inventory
maps every SQL Server SPN.

562
00:38:06.853 --> 00:38:11.807
In the program's experience a missing one
is the most common cause of silent NTLM

563
00:38:11.807 --> 00:38:15.336
and a duplicate the second,
and an instance under a domain

564
00:38:15.336 --> 00:38:19.465
account with no SPN usually means
registration failed at startup.

565
00:38:19.465 --> 00:38:25.655
The D1.7 collision report classifies five
collision types and marks as critical any

566
00:38:25.655 --> 00:38:31.315
SPN held by two or more enabled accounts,
or by both a computer and a user object.

567
00:38:31.315 --> 00:38:34.325
Run it against Active Directory directly,

568
00:38:34.325 --> 00:38:38.453
because its input-file handoff
has the same defect as R03's.

569
00:38:38.453 --> 00:38:44.277
And D1.4 finds delegation settings
and NTLM-dependency indicators.

570
00:38:44.277 --> 00:38:48.409
Remediation changes production,
and the program's SPN plan

571
00:38:48.409 --> 00:38:52.540
and GPO report enforce nothing;
they emit reviewable plans.

572
00:38:52.540 --> 00:38:58.040
Register a missing SPN with SetSPN
dash S, in the fully qualified form.

573
00:38:58.040 --> 00:39:02.852
Repair a collision by removing the SPN
from the disabled or wrong account,

574
00:39:02.852 --> 00:39:05.140
leaving exactly one enabled owner.

575
00:39:05.140 --> 00:39:07.026
For linked-server passthrough,

576
00:39:07.026 --> 00:39:11.233
the order of preference is Entra
authentication on the linked server,

577
00:39:11.233 --> 00:39:15.513
then Kerberos with constrained
delegation, then a mapped remote login

578
00:39:15.513 --> 00:39:19.865
only as a documented exception, because
it brings back a stored password.

579
00:39:19.865 --> 00:39:24.369
Then the Group Policy phases: level
five refuses LM and version 1;

580
00:39:24.369 --> 00:39:27.684
the Restrict NTLM policies
restrict version 2,

581
00:39:27.684 --> 00:39:32.529
with server-name exceptions at the
domain controller and the client;

582
00:39:32.529 --> 00:39:36.778
and the full block comes on the
program's April 2027 backstop.

583
00:39:36.778 --> 00:39:41.953
The GPO report classifies each policy's
posture and never sets anything.

584
00:39:41.953 --> 00:39:44.843
Verify with klist purge,
reconnect and klist,

585
00:39:44.843 --> 00:39:49.140
and check that the auth scheme
reads KERBEROS from a remote client.

586
00:39:49.140 --> 00:39:53.985
Rollback is SetSPN dash D and the
prior policy values, and because the

587
00:39:53.985 --> 00:39:59.700
block is last, the Kerberos fixes are
proven long before anything is refused.

588
00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:04.187
Runbook R07 moves the
access-control layer.

589
00:40:04.187 --> 00:40:08.478
The group model survives; what
changes is who maintains membership,

590
00:40:08.478 --> 00:40:12.538
who may administer it, and the
boundary every sign-in has to cross.

591
00:40:12.538 --> 00:40:14.225
Phase 1 is read-only.

592
00:40:14.225 --> 00:40:18.600
The program's group export reads
Entra groups through Microsoft Graph

593
00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:23.050
or replays a snapshot, and the dynamic
switch captures membership rules.

594
00:40:23.050 --> 00:40:26.850
A GCC tenant keeps the
worldwide Graph endpoint.

595
00:40:26.850 --> 00:40:30.522
The Conditional Access export
confirms the perimeter,

596
00:40:30.522 --> 00:40:33.795
but a policy's state only
says on, off or report-only;

597
00:40:33.795 --> 00:40:39.063
whether it covers SQL Server sign-ins
depends on its users and target resources.

598
00:40:39.063 --> 00:40:43.088
The classification function gives
every group one of four types.

599
00:40:43.088 --> 00:40:46.223
Type 1, organizational unit (OU)-derived,

600
00:40:46.223 --> 00:40:50.338
becomes a dynamic security
group and a group login.

601
00:40:50.338 --> 00:40:55.613
Type 2, explicit role assignment, becomes
an assigned group and a group login.

602
00:40:55.613 --> 00:40:57.911
Type 3, a distribution list,

603
00:40:57.911 --> 00:41:03.137
becomes a Microsoft 365 group
with no SQL Server login at all.

604
00:41:03.137 --> 00:41:08.212
Type 4, mail-enabled security, splits
into a mail group and an access group,

605
00:41:08.212 --> 00:41:10.749
and only the access half gets a login.

606
00:41:10.749 --> 00:41:13.438
Group logins are type X, not type E.

607
00:41:13.438 --> 00:41:17.187
Phase 2 is review-required,
and its order is fixed.

608
00:41:17.187 --> 00:41:21.135
First the DBA administrative
unit, with restricted management

609
00:41:21.135 --> 00:41:24.487
turned on at creation, because
it can't be changed later.

610
00:41:24.487 --> 00:41:28.680
Second, Groups Administrator scoped
to that unit for the DBA lead,

611
00:41:28.680 --> 00:41:31.475
eligible through PIM rather than standing.

612
00:41:31.475 --> 00:41:36.226
Third, the groups inside it, added
explicitly, with logins generated by the

613
00:41:36.226 --> 00:41:40.900
group-login script, which can never
turn a distribution list into a login.

614
00:41:40.900 --> 00:41:46.075
Know the costs: tenant administrators can
still assign themselves over the unit,

615
00:41:46.075 --> 00:41:49.473
though it's audited; its
members can't be covered by PIM,

616
00:41:49.473 --> 00:41:54.725
access reviews or entitlement management;
and a tenant gets at most 100 such units.

617
00:41:54.725 --> 00:41:58.127
The dividend: once every
login is an Entra principal,

618
00:41:58.127 --> 00:42:01.688
Conditional Access is the
perimeter for human access.

619
00:42:01.688 --> 00:42:06.924
Microsoft documents targeting the Azure
SQL Database application; that it governs

620
00:42:06.924 --> 00:42:12.012
Arc-enabled SQL Server is the program's
working assumption, to confirm in the pilot.

621
00:42:12.012 --> 00:42:16.080
It reaches users only, and
risk conditions need P2.

622
00:42:16.830 --> 00:42:19.493
Runbook R08 is the last mile.

623
00:42:19.493 --> 00:42:24.310
An application that signs users in with
Entra ID but reaches the database with

624
00:42:24.310 --> 00:42:28.980
a stored password has moved its front
door and left the back door where it was.

625
00:42:28.980 --> 00:42:35.280
Phase 1 inventories the LDAP-bind
dependency with the read-only D1.9 script.

626
00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:42.911
It combines account heuristics with domain
controller evidence: event 4624 with

627
00:42:42.911 --> 00:42:45.604
logon type 8, a clear-text bind,

628
00:42:45.604 --> 00:42:50.542
and events 2887 and 2889 in
the Directory Service log.

629
00:42:50.542 --> 00:42:52.552
Event 2889 names each client,

630
00:42:52.552 --> 00:42:57.491
but only after the LDAP interface
diagnostic setting is raised to two,

631
00:42:57.491 --> 00:43:00.086
and nothing is logged retroactively,

632
00:43:00.086 --> 00:43:04.355
so start the window after the
change and set it back afterward.

633
00:43:04.355 --> 00:43:07.725
D1.9 doesn't say which
instance an application uses,

634
00:43:07.725 --> 00:43:13.021
so the program's connection-plan function
joins it to the D1.8 audit, assigns each

635
00:43:13.021 --> 00:43:18.718
application a class from observable signals,
and emits the connection-string change.

636
00:43:18.718 --> 00:43:21.968
Phase 2 is the simplest
change and the most gated,

637
00:43:21.968 --> 00:43:26.030
and it's made in application
configuration, never by a script.

638
00:43:26.030 --> 00:43:31.067
In production, name a specific mode, such
as Active Directory Managed Identity.

639
00:43:31.067 --> 00:43:34.425
The Default mode tries credential
providers in sequence,

640
00:43:34.425 --> 00:43:39.042
which slows the first connection and can
switch identity when the host changes.

641
00:43:39.042 --> 00:43:41.823
Service principal mode
takes its secret from

642
00:43:41.823 --> 00:43:44.530
protected configuration,
never from source.

643
00:43:44.530 --> 00:43:49.468
Setting the mode turns on encryption, so
the server certificate must be trusted.

644
00:43:49.468 --> 00:43:54.166
On an Arc host the driver gets the machine's
token from the local metadata service,

645
00:43:54.166 --> 00:43:58.443
and the process must be in the hybrid
agent extension applications group.

646
00:43:58.443 --> 00:44:02.048
Every application on that host
is then the same principal,

647
00:44:02.048 --> 00:44:05.729
so give each its own service
principal where grants differ.

648
00:44:05.729 --> 00:44:10.769
Three gates before cutover: the Entra
login exists, the identity holds the right

649
00:44:10.769 --> 00:44:15.655
database role, and the change was tested
on a production-equivalent Arc stack.

650
00:44:15.655 --> 00:44:20.175
The audience is database.windows.net,
not the Azure Government one,

651
00:44:20.175 --> 00:44:23.955
and pooled applications use
the access-token callback.

652
00:44:23.955 --> 00:44:26.511
Rollback is the prior connection string,

653
00:44:26.511 --> 00:44:29.580
with the legacy login
disabled, not dropped.

654
00:44:30.330 --> 00:44:33.455
Runbook R09 keeps proving the posture.

655
00:44:33.455 --> 00:44:35.716
Closure isn't a day on the calendar;

656
00:44:35.716 --> 00:44:40.643
it's a record that keeps coming back
clean, and everything here is read-only.

657
00:44:40.643 --> 00:44:45.379
Step one runs the D1.8 deep audit
on a schedule against the retain

658
00:44:45.379 --> 00:44:50.205
and target estate, keeping every
run's JSON under version control.

659
00:44:50.205 --> 00:44:54.093
It's the same script as R03,
on a different cadence.

660
00:44:54.093 --> 00:44:58.305
Step two projects each run onto
the per-instance closure record.

661
00:44:58.305 --> 00:45:01.440
The fields are Windows
Authentication mode,

662
00:45:01.440 --> 00:45:04.661
which proves only that
SQL logins are refused;

663
00:45:04.661 --> 00:45:08.559
zero active SQL logins outside
the exception registry;

664
00:45:08.559 --> 00:45:10.931
zero active user and group logins;

665
00:45:10.931 --> 00:45:16.185
every Entra login reconciled to a known
principal; SA disabled and renamed;

666
00:45:16.185 --> 00:45:20.337
the Arc agent connected; Entra
authentication configured,

667
00:45:20.337 --> 00:45:25.083
through the managed identity on 2025
or an app registration on 2022;

668
00:45:25.083 --> 00:45:30.930
and MFA through Conditional Access, joined
from the sign-in logs rather than copied.

669
00:45:30.930 --> 00:45:34.464
The program's closure-record
function returns closed,

670
00:45:34.464 --> 00:45:37.605
closed-excepted or open,
with the failing fields.

671
00:45:37.605 --> 00:45:42.377
Step three turns the difference
between two runs into drift events:

672
00:45:42.377 --> 00:45:47.830
mixed mode enabled, a new SQL login, a new
Windows login or the Arc agent offline,

673
00:45:47.830 --> 00:45:51.920
each a violation, plus instances
that appear or disappear,

674
00:45:51.920 --> 00:45:53.880
which may be a discovery gap.

675
00:45:53.880 --> 00:45:56.865
One caution: the audit
reads the running mode,

676
00:45:56.865 --> 00:46:00.980
so a registry change is invisible
until the service restarts.

677
00:46:00.980 --> 00:46:04.018
Add a registry check if
earlier warning matters.

678
00:46:04.018 --> 00:46:08.492
Step four is the behavioral layer,
three sources with distinct jobs.

679
00:46:08.492 --> 00:46:13.131
Defender for SQL raises threat alerts
but isn't an authentication log.

680
00:46:13.131 --> 00:46:16.119
Entra sign-in logs record
each token issued,

681
00:46:16.119 --> 00:46:19.192
and a token can be reused
across connections.

682
00:46:19.192 --> 00:46:22.693
The instance and host
record the protocol itself,

683
00:46:22.693 --> 00:46:26.792
and any NTLM or Kerberos
connection is an immediate alert.

684
00:46:26.792 --> 00:46:32.263
The transformation is closed when six
evidence sets hold together across the

685
00:46:32.263 --> 00:46:37.059
validation period: closure records,
sign-in logs, protocol telemetry,

686
00:46:37.059 --> 00:46:40.930
the SA report, the MFA report
and the exception registry.

687
00:46:40.930 --> 00:46:45.480
Then April 1, 2027 arrives as a non-event.

688
00:46:46.230 --> 00:46:50.405
Runbook R10 is discovery and
analysis, not provisioning.

689
00:46:50.405 --> 00:46:54.255
Before anyone sets a date to switch
off the certificate authority,

690
00:46:54.255 --> 00:46:56.705
someone has to find out
what it still signs.

691
00:46:56.705 --> 00:47:01.029
The program has no decision yet on
the move from AD CS to a cloud PKI,

692
00:47:01.029 --> 00:47:05.193
so this runbook produces the
evidence that decision will need.

693
00:47:05.193 --> 00:47:10.601
Step one runs the read-only D1.10
certificate audit across Active Directory:

694
00:47:10.601 --> 00:47:16.268
the PKI inventory, machine and user
certificates, smart-card-required accounts,

695
00:47:16.268 --> 00:47:21.592
and service dependencies, including
LDAP over TLS on domain controllers.

696
00:47:21.592 --> 00:47:24.952
It records the issuing
CA on every certificate,

697
00:47:24.952 --> 00:47:28.655
which is exactly the signal
for what depends on AD CS.

698
00:47:28.655 --> 00:47:33.080
It doesn't reach into an instance, so
three in-engine reads supplement it.

699
00:47:33.080 --> 00:47:36.143
Join certificates to
their logins on the SID,

700
00:47:36.143 --> 00:47:39.205
not the principal ID,
and read the issuer name.

701
00:47:39.205 --> 00:47:44.004
An empty certificate setting doesn't mean
no certificate, because SQL Server picks

702
00:47:44.004 --> 00:47:48.455
one from the machine store or generates
its own; check the error log at startup.

703
00:47:48.455 --> 00:47:52.538
And check mirroring and
availability-group endpoints;

704
00:47:52.538 --> 00:47:55.555
one reporting NTLM is also an R06 finding.

705
00:47:55.555 --> 00:47:58.993
Step two maps the results
to three dependencies.

706
00:47:58.993 --> 00:48:03.577
The engine's TLS certificate: every
Entra connection requires encryption,

707
00:48:03.577 --> 00:48:06.534
so if the CA is gone when
the certificate expires,

708
00:48:06.534 --> 00:48:09.492
encryption becomes what
breaks the connection.

709
00:48:09.492 --> 00:48:14.226
Certificate-mapped logins and endpoints
carry less risk than the source claimed,

710
00:48:14.226 --> 00:48:17.150
because certificate logins
are used only for code

711
00:48:17.150 --> 00:48:20.004
signing and can't connect,
and most are self-signed.

712
00:48:20.004 --> 00:48:25.495
And certificate-based sign-in, the
interim posture for the NTLM long tail,

713
00:48:25.495 --> 00:48:29.430
needs a replacement CA
issuing before AD CS retires.

714
00:48:29.430 --> 00:48:33.672
Step three orders the CA migration,
roots before issuing CAs,

715
00:48:33.672 --> 00:48:38.168
with cycles and cross-domain
dependencies flagged as blockers.

716
00:48:38.168 --> 00:48:43.499
One fact for the decision: Microsoft Cloud
PKI issues to Intune-managed devices,

717
00:48:43.499 --> 00:48:46.326
and Intune doesn't support Windows Server,

718
00:48:46.326 --> 00:48:50.042
so SQL Server hosts would
need another enrollment path.

719
00:48:50.042 --> 00:48:51.903
The finding is one question:

720
00:48:51.903 --> 00:48:56.029
does the AD CS decommission run
earlier than its replacement?

721
00:48:56.029 --> 00:49:00.400
If it does, the interim posture
is lost exactly when it's needed.

722
00:49:01.150 --> 00:49:04.100
To close, five things to
take back to the estate.

723
00:49:04.100 --> 00:49:06.312
First, count before you migrate.

724
00:49:06.312 --> 00:49:08.084
The inventory and the retain,

725
00:49:08.084 --> 00:49:13.028
consolidate or retire gate decide where
every hour of authentication work goes;

726
00:49:13.028 --> 00:49:17.676
migrating an instance you should have
retired is waste against a fixed date.

727
00:49:17.676 --> 00:49:20.113
Second, measure, don't assume.

728
00:49:20.113 --> 00:49:23.801
A missing SPN turns Kerberos
into NTLM silently,

729
00:49:23.801 --> 00:49:29.612
and only the auth scheme column, the
Windows logs and SQL Server Audit show it.

730
00:49:29.612 --> 00:49:31.550
Third, keep it reversible.

731
00:49:31.550 --> 00:49:36.575
Create, validate, disable, observe
for thirty days, then drop.

732
00:49:36.575 --> 00:49:39.312
A disabled login is an instant rollback.

733
00:49:39.312 --> 00:49:40.975
Fourth, finish the chain.

734
00:49:40.975 --> 00:49:45.698
An instance isn't closed while any
application still reaches it with Integrated

735
00:49:45.698 --> 00:49:47.461
Security or a stored password,

736
00:49:47.461 --> 00:49:51.338
or while a Class 4 application
holds it on Windows Authentication.

737
00:49:51.338 --> 00:49:53.399
Fifth, prove it continuously.

738
00:49:53.399 --> 00:49:58.318
The closure record, drift detection and
the six evidence sets make done a queryable

739
00:49:58.318 --> 00:50:01.525
posture rather than a claim,
and the certificate layer

740
00:50:01.525 --> 00:50:04.875
underneath needs its own
decision before AD CS goes away.

741
00:50:04.875 --> 00:50:08.825
April 1, 2027 is the program's backstop.

742
00:50:08.825 --> 00:50:12.151
If the work is finished well
before it and the evidence

743
00:50:12.151 --> 00:50:15.625
has been clean for months, the
block arrives as a non-event.

744
00:50:15.625 --> 00:50:19.746
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 06 has the narrative

745
00:50:19.746 --> 00:50:24.100
chapters and the ten runbooks, with
every external claim footnoted.

746
00:50:24.100 --> 00:50:24.930
Thank you.
